 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfjuqdCJ0Qx/",
 "title": "Why leaders should not point out mistakes directly",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that directly pointing out people's mistakes makes them feel attacked and defensive rather than helping them improve. Instead, leaders should ask questions to understand the situation, because people who feel understood are more willing to accept feedback.",
 "key_points": [
  "Never point out the problems of people around you, especially when you are a leader.",
  "The more you try to correct someone, the less they want to listen to you.",
  "Direct criticism makes employees feel stupid and attacked, causing them to defend themselves instead of improving.",
  "When corrected harshly, employees may become quiet, start arguing, or eventually stop listening entirely.",
  "Before correcting someone, try to understand them by asking three questions: What happened? What makes you decide to do it in this way? What would you do differently next time?",
  "When people feel understood, they are more willing to accept feedback.",
  "A leader's job is not to prove they are right, but to help their people become better."
 ],
 "content": "Never Point out the problem of the people around you, when you are a leader. Now you might thinking, oh, if I don't point out their mistake, how are they going to improve? But here's something that you need to really understand. The more you try to correct someone, the lesser they want to listen to you. Let me give you an example. Imagine you just have an employee who makes a mistake. You immediately tell them, hey, why did you do this in that way, huh? You have already told you so many times, you have to do it in this way. Why can't you just follow the instruction? And you think you are helping them to improve. But what do they feel? They feel stupid, they feel attacked, and instead of thinking about how to improve themselves, they start thinking about how to defend themselves. That's why some employee become quiet, some start to argue with you, and some eventually stop listening to you. So, here's what I want you to do instead. Before you correct someone, try to understand them. Ask them this question. What happened? What makes you decide to do it in this way? What would you do differently next time? Because when people feel understood, they are more willing to accept feedback. And remember, your job as a leader is not to prove that you are right. Your job as a leader is to help your people to become a better person. Follow me if you want to learn about leadership in healthcare and communication in healthcare. Text shown on screen: Never Point out the problem of the people.",
